Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/1QzlcGem-2k/m/ZzM_8inV8FoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Will,

I believe the error message here is accurate—the way you've written
it, p(Z|...) is discontinuous, due to the step function. This causes
problems for NUTS, which really wants a continuous (and ideally
continuously differentiable) log-posterior.

An alternative approach is to declare the latent Z variables to be
constrained to be consistent with the observed data, like so:

parameters {
  real<lower=0> Zarray[n, J];

  real B[J, K];
  cov_matrix[J] S;
}

transformed parameters {
  # This is a workaround to deal with the fact
  # that we don't yet support bounded vectors
  vector[J] Z[n];

  for (i in 1:n)
    for (j in 1:J)

      Z[n, j] <- Zarray[n, J];
}
model {
  ...

  for (i in 1:n) {
    for (j in 1:J) {

      Mu[i, j] <- (2*Y[i,j]-1) * dot_product(B[j], X[i]);

    }
  }

  S ~ inv_wishart(df, ID);

  for (i in 1:n) {
    Z[i] ~ multi_normal(col(Mu', i), S);
  }
}

It's a little roundabout, but (unless I messed up somewhere) this
model is equivalent to the one you wrote.

I've actually had some trouble getting multivariate probit models to
converge in a reasonable amount of time in Stan, so I'd be very
interested to hear if you have better luck than I did (using a
slightly different approach).

Best,
