On 9/21/12 2:04 PM, Josef Fruehwald wrote:> |
 > data{
 >      int<lower = 0> N;           // number of observations
 >      real y[N];                  // y

...

Thanks for sending the model.  In the version you sent me,
I'm not getting an issue with C++, but rather with parsing:

============================================================

--- Translating Stan graphical model to C++ code ---
bin/stanc /Users/carp/temp2/fruehwald.stan --o=/Users/carp/temp2/fruehwald.cpp
Model name=fruehwald_model
Input file=/Users/carp/temp2/fruehwald.stan
Output file=/Users/carp/temp2/fruehwald.cpp

ERROR PARSING
LOCATION:  file=input; line=61, column=36

         Delta[i,1,1] <- sum(delta[1:i]);

                                    ^-- here

DIAGNOSTIC(S) FROM PARSER:

Parser expecting: "]"

=============================================================

Stan doesn't support an R-style : operation inside
of indexes.  We're thinking about adding that option
some day, but it's not among our top priorities yet.

The loop is also problematic in terms of efficiency.
It winds up being quadratic because it does summations
for vectors of size 1:max_dob, which takes on
order max_dob * max_dob operations.
So I commented it out:

  // for(i in 1:max_dob){
     //     Delta[i,1,1] <- sum(delta[1:i]);
     //     Delta[i,1,2] <- sum(c2_delta[1:i]);
     //     Delta[i,2,1] <- sum(male_delta[1:i]);
     //     Delta[i,2,2] <- sum(c2_male_delta[1:i]);
     // }

and went with the way you had commented out:

     Delta[1,1,1] <- delta[1];

     Delta[1,1,2] <- delta[1] + c2_delta[1];

     Delta[1,2,1] <- delta[1] + male_delta[1];

     Delta[1,2,2] <- delta[1] + c2_delta[1] + male_delta[1] + c2_male_delta[1];

     for(i in 2:max_dob){

        Delta[i,1,1] <- Delta[i-1,1,1] + delta[i];

        Delta[i,1,2] <- Delta[i-1,1,2] + delta[i] + c2_delta[i];

        Delta[i,2,1] <- Delta[i-1,2,1] + delta[i] + male_delta[i];

        Delta[i,2,2] <- Delta[i-1,2,2] + delta[i] + c2_delta[i] + male_delta[i] + c2_male_delta[i];
     }

That version works on my machine, though your mileage
may vary.

There may be a problem with the version of C++ you're
using.  Older versions have trouble with some of the
template metaprograms, so could raise errors we're not
anticipating.

- Bob

