Thanks for the dumps.  I don't think this is the problem as
I recreated a standalone unit test which seems to work just
fine (see below and test/agrad/matrix_test.cpp in the repository).

I don't know what to do in the face of large, complex
user models like this one.  I just don't have the time to
spend debugging.

One thing users can do to help is to try to isolate
where the problem is and create a small model that causes
the problem.  I know this can be hard, but without it,
it's very hard for me to dive into these big models.

Before getting back to the case at hand,
I want to recommend that users (including me when I'm
using Stan) should build models up stepwise from simple to
complex.  That lets you start with something that works,
then gradually add new effects, new hierarchical levels,
new priors, etc.  A diff between a model that worked and
the one step more complex model that doesn't is invaluable
for debugging.

The print statements coming in Stan 1.0.2 should help in
these models with complex assignments, too.

The seg fault could come about if one of the variables in
the matrix was not initialized (then the .vi_->val will
cause a segfault) or we somehow to tried to index beyond the
size of either v1 or v2 in v1[i] and v2[i]:

Program received signal SIGSEGV, Segmentation fault.
0x000000000040f565 in stan::agrad::(anonymous
namespace)::dot_product_vv_vari::var_dot<Eigen::Block<Eigen::Matrix<stan::agrad::var, -1, -1, 0, -1, -1> const, 1, -1,
false, true>, Eigen::Block<Eigen::Matrix<stan::agrad::var, -1, 1, 0, -1, 1> const, -1, 1, true, true> > (v1=..., v2=...)
at src/stan/agrad/matrix.hpp:672
672                 result += v1[i].vi_->val_ * v2[i].vi_->val_;
(gdb) bt

I created an independent unit test in test/agrad/matrix_test.cpp,
in which I recreated the set of ops that fail in the user model,
but made sure everything was initialized properly.

So I'm guessing something's not getting defined somewhere in the
user model.  As soon as prints are in for 1.0.2, this kind of
thing should be easier to debug.

Here's the new test case:

TEST(AgradMatrix, UserCase1) {
   using std::vector;
   using stan::math::multiply;
   using stan::math::transpose;
   using stan::math::subtract;
   using stan::math::get_base1;
   using stan::agrad::assign;
   using stan::math::dot_product;

   size_t H = 2;
   size_t DpKm1 = 4;

   vector_v vk(DpKm1);
   for (size_t k = 0; k < DpKm1; ++k)
     vk[k] = (k + 1) * (k + 2);

   matrix_v L_etaprec(DpKm1,DpKm1);
   for (size_t m = 0; m < DpKm1; ++m)
     for (size_t n = 0; n < DpKm1; ++n)
       L_etaprec(m,n) = (m + 1) * (n + 1);

   vector_d etamu(DpKm1);
   for (size_t k = 0; k < DpKm1; ++k)
     etamu[k] = 10 + (k * k);

   vector<vector_d> eta(H,vector_d(DpKm1));
   for (size_t h = 0; h < H; ++h)
     for (size_t k = 0; k < DpKm1; ++k)
       eta[h][k] = (h + 1) * (k + 10);

   var lp__ = 0.0;

   for (size_t h = 1; h <= H; ++h) {

     assign(vk, multiply(transpose(L_etaprec),
                         subtract(get_base1(eta,h,"eta",1),
                                  etamu)));
     assign(lp__, (lp__ - (0.5 * dot_product(vk,vk))));
   }

   EXPECT_TRUE(lp__.val() != 0);
}

